@startuml d8_flattened_nofk
!include _style.puml
title D8 — flattened-nofk  (D3 with referential integrity switched off)

entity "charity" as c {
  * charity_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * name, country, founded_on
}
entity "person" as p {
  * person_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  <back:#F5D0D0>* charity_id : BIGINT  — plain column, no FK</back>
  * full_name, email, joined_at
}
entity "donation" as d {
  * donation_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  <back:#F5D0D0>* person_id : BIGINT  — plain column, no FK</back>
  <back:#F5D0D0>* charity_id : BIGINT  — plain column, no FK</back>
  * amount_cents, currency, donated_at, note
}
c ..o{ p : "convention only"
p ..o{ d : "convention only"

note bottom of d
  **Identical to D3 in every other respect:** same columns,
  same five indexes, same queries, same write statements.
  The D3/D8 pair isolates one variable — the cost of
  enforcing referential integrity.

  **What a foreign key does on every INSERT:**
   1. look up the parent person row
   2. look up the parent charity row
   3. take a KEY SHARE lock on each, held to commit

  So a "single-row insert" is three row accesses and two
  shared locks — on rows other concurrent inserts are
  touching too. On PostgreSQL those are local buffer reads.
  <back:#FFE0E0>On YugabyteDB the parent may live on another node,</back>
  <back:#FFE0E0>so each check can become a cross-node RPC.</back>

  **What you give up:** nothing prevents a donation pointing
  at a person who does not exist, and nothing keeps
  donation.charity_id agreeing with person.charity_id.
  The numbers say what that risk is worth.
end note
@enduml
